{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1427797-advice-for-spirit-antagonist-creation","id":1427797,"title":"Advice for Spirit Antagonist Creation","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-12-08T19:29:46-08:00","id":1427797,"title":"Advice for Spirit Antagonist Creation","content":"I've started a Werewolf: The Forsaken game, 2nd Edition. I'm looking for advice on how to build Spirits as antagonists and allies. I could go into a lot of detail about the specifics of the story I'm doing and all that, but what I'm really looking for is suggestions regarding mechanics of the game to provide interesting encounters for the players. Whether challenging fights, contests of wits, chases across the Shadow-scape or whatever I need them for.<br /> <br /> Does anyone know of resources providing suggestions and tutorials on how to build Spirit NPCs? Are there any builds for accomplishing specific kinds of outcomes (like combat-monster able to face a Rahu)? <br /> <br /> Does anyone just have some solid advice on how to start, what to focus on, or even how to approach building Spirit NPCs that they've learned from doing it themselves?<br /> <br /> I appreciate any suggestions and thank you very much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/851-paradim","name":"Paradim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-09T14:47:09-08:00","id":1427909,"title":"","content":"Asa first step I generally look at the characters\u2019 abilities in whatever field I\u2019m looking to challenge, figure out the average successes they can expect with dice pool/3, and then start building the antagonist to match that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-09T21:40:23-08:00","id":1427941,"title":"","content":"I was thinking about this for a while then reread what you wanted. I don't have much advice for a race, but I can direct you to the race rules and rules for how to make spirits, as well as the more obvious race-related Numina. I do totally second Bunyip's advice, just knowing the math can help you build things that can seem dangerous but can't beat the players and will keep you from making a BBEG who will die in one round.<br /> <br /> Just a few tips before getting into the more direct advice.<ul><li>Numina are generic, feel free to add them to make them as specific and fitting as possible. The powers of the other splats can sometimes be good for this. Beast's Atavisms especially.</li> <li>Influences are storyteller fiat powers, the ST's going to have to do a lot of the legwork there but basically there's no reason to softball it, spirits can be powerful and a Rank 5 spirit of fire is basically a god of fire, so 'but that's too powerful' probably shouldn't come up. If you're having trouble with figuring out capabilities, Falco wrote a great supplement about it on the STV called <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/265598/Ephemeral-Influence\" target=\"_blank\">Ephemeral Influence, found here</a>.</li> <li>As always, know your characters capabilities. An Ithaeur's Moon Gifts can turn the table against spirits pretty fast. Simply knowing a PC's Health and Defense can help you figure out proper dicepools that can damage but not necessarily kill them.</li> <li>There's a tendency to see the Shadow as a broad expanse with groups of tiny pointless spirits, except with big powerful spirits lingering in other places alone. If you have that in mind, toss it out. Spirits are social creatures. They have minions, they often work in packs even at high rank. A powerful spirit can have a lot of smaller spirits, mid-rank lieutenant spirits, or similar-rank partners. This helps offset the traditional action economy problems of a 5 PC pack vs 1 spirit, which favors the pack. Keeping this in mind, you can let Gauru use their Down &amp; Dirty to wipe out swarms of Rank 1s.</li> <li>Ephemera Ranks do not increase linearly. A Rank 1 spirit is like an animal, a Rank 3 spirit like a mortal+ or chargen supernatural, a Rank 5 spirit is more in line with a Power Stat 10 supernatural.</li> <li>The ephemera mechanics start around p183 in the book, it's all pretty simple. The Sparkblood Seneschal on p201-202 is a good example of the type of spirit that would have minions, while the shadow wraith on the same page is more like a minion, but can be threatening on its own to the weak and unsuspecting.</li> </ul>Pretty much all the mechanics for chases, and investigations, are with combat, in the Chronicles of Darkness core. They're pretty simple. But that's not really going to spice up the game. All the Numina and Dread Powers can be seen on <a href=\"http://wodcodex.com/wiki/Numina\" target=\"_blank\">WoDCodex</a>. The Speed Numina lets you double or triple your Speed. Pathfinder lets you know the quickest path to a given destination. They're both in the core, and Werewolf.<br /> <br /> In terms of dealing with spirits, to make that more interesting, not everything has to be about death. A fetish can act as a prison for a spirit, and one can save a lot of Health by convincing a spirit that it may be better off in that fetish than it would be outside. You can also use shadowbind to lock them to a place without even having to fight one (though it helps to have someone fighting them while you do it).<br /> <br /> tl;dr advice to help go beyond<br /> The trick is appreciating that each scene can be more than a mechanical solution. Scenes aren't just a series of rolls, they have reasons and repercussions for happening. Plus if you have a group of players, and you decide to race The Wind, only one of them is actually going to stand a chance and having the other players are going to sit there waiting for their turn to come. Just like video games have made it so that you have to do more than place your character at one spot at the end of the level, and how even now just shooting the Big Bad has been expanded upon in a wide variety of ways, usually breaking down fights into phases, we can do the same thing. Especially when we keep in mind a fight, a race, a romantic encounter, or trying to convince the Guardian of the Tome that you really need to see the Tome are all these scenes that can be built for more enjoyment.<br /> <br /> Por ejemplo, the Sparkblood Seneschal is a big boss, he's not going to join a race. But he likely has messenger spirits, entities of information, electricity, and light. Things that travel fast. These will be his racers. But a pure foot-race against a werewolf solves nothing, what if rather than racing to one point and back, they have to do something at that point, then get back (either that thing has to be widely obvious proof it's been done, or they have to carry it back). This allows three spots for the werewolves to do something. One to get there fast, the next to destroy a rival spirit or break into a building and cut off its power, then three to get back to the Seneschal in the chaos resulting from the second one (you just blacked out a quarter of the city! and as you escape, you see another chunk go dark, the other spirits must have succeeded too, maybe time to split up and let the combat or spirit-dealers ambush those spirits while your speediest gets back to the Seneschal).<br /> <br /> This guy gives great advice about how to spice up Encounters. He runs D&amp;D so they're Encounters but they are effectively Scenes in Chronicles. The mechanics, for the most part, are going to stay there but there's options that can be brought over pretty easily. His introductions are a bit long and exaggerated, but once he gets into talking about games he's dissected the hows and whys better than anyone else I've read. Here's a series of articles all about making better battles.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/\" target=\"_blank\"> Page not found | The Angry GM </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/\" target=\"_blank\"> https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> The Angry GM delivers advice to players and dungeon masters of fantasy role-playing games with humor, snark, and attitude. Game masters and players are sure to find something of use, whether they are playing AD&amp;D, D&amp;D 3.5, D&amp;D 4E, 5E, Pathfinder, D&amp;D Next, or any other role-playing game. </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Here's one that can be brought over. He suggests a creature can get an extra turn to noticeably 'power up' and it leads to their ability. Like a dragon inhales, the temperature suddenly rises, and next turn it can use its breath weapon, giving players a way to be aware of when these big whammies are coming. We don't have a specific 'ultra power' of spirits as is, but it would be easy to make one. Especially when using Claimed, who can have basically any power you want (don't be afraid to mimic powers of Beasts or Prometheans, if it's appropriate). But even without designing a new Numina we can still use the idea. See, I never liked the idea of Willpower with spirits. So what if instead of that, they could just power up, and the next turn whatever inherent ability they do (physical attack, Numina, or Influence) they get +Rank to their roll.<br /> <a href=\"https://theangrygm.com/barking-at-your-players-advanced-combat-narration/\" target=\"_blank\">https://theangrygm.com/barking-at-yo...bat-narration/</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":".  Gotta give credit to Vargos, who recommended the book this time. 12-10-2020, 12:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-13T07:15:44-08:00","id":1428266,"title":"","content":"Regarding the Shadow Wraith, if a spirit can boost its traits by rank +2, the shadow wraith can get its dice pool of power plus finesse to 18, I would also say its rending talons probably offer a +2 damage bonus so I would say that they can be a menace to Urania ( I just ran a Exalted game so there were buckets of dice, thank goodness for the dice roller app). That would seem like a pretty potent challenge.<br /> <br /> in addition, if a spirit possesses a body and that vessel gets shredded in combat, can the same spirit still re-possess, the same vessel a corpse? I would imagine this would be a good reason not to go into Kuruth around the claimed since the spirit could find a new vessel after the rampage ends. Another reason to think twice about Kuruth<br /> <br /> Finally, regarding the Awe Numina, if the Senechal uses Awe and achieves an Exceptional success ( not unlikely given its dice pool), would the Uratha be completely frozen for 3 turns, including being unable to change forms? That would be frightening if it gets a free 3 turns to act while the pack is frozen. Just wondering","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}